- Before proposing new work, the user checks what already exists — existing solutions/catalogs, open issues, and PRs — to leverage prior art over building from scratch, and drafts before filing upstream issues (verifying the issue is not already in discussion and that the target repo has no policy against AI-authored issues). Leverage-over-build and draft-before-file are the recurring habits. The user also anticipates how an LLM analyzer will read an upstream filing and wants it self-defending — preempting objections in-body rather than relying on a human asking. Upstream filings are held to a strict evidence standard: every claim must carry a live-log excerpt (not just source reading), the diff must be minimal and scoped to the active path only (not full patch history), and the base commit must be stated explicitly. The user audits externally-facing artifacts (PR bodies, issue text) for evidence integrity — catching when quoted 'verbatim' content has been silently transformed — and expects maintainer review items to be implemented immediately once surfaced, not left as proposals awaiting approval. The user expects existing design documents and prior work products to be consulted and merged before implementing, becoming frustrated when the agent re-derives from scratch instead of merging settled decisions, and prefers porting existing work over writing new when prior work exists. Redundancy is identified by asking whether two systems can share the same values, driving unification rather than coexistence. Before deciding whether to file upstream, the user probes implementation feasibility ('how would it be implemented') as an inquiry, not a directive to build.
- The user is cost-conscious and rejects over-engineering for one-off tasks: they prefer the simplest working path over elaborate custom setups (no image pushes, no WebUI tinkery), choose cheap fetch methods (webfetch/curl) and escalate to a browser only for bot-protected pages, pick the cheapest resource offers, and expect dead/burning instances to be destroyed immediately rather than left running. When a web source is bot-walled, they save the page locally and upload the HTML as primary-source evidence rather than asking the assistant to find a fetch workaround. Cost consciousness extends to paid GPU rental time: all offline preparation (reading, design, cascade checks) is done before renting, and when the agent reaches for an expensive re-fetch the user points to the cheaper in-place alternative. On long-horizon build, port, or measurement tasks the user tracks elapsed cost (rental-box hours, session budget) and challenges the return on it, expecting the cheapest discriminating check surfaced early (does the binary even start, does the gate block it) before burning hours on expensive builds, and expects time spent versus progress stated at checkpoints rather than having to ask.
- The user expects the assistant to complete all verification before reporting completion or asking for input — including A/B testing for bug fixes (revert → confirm bug → re-apply → confirm fix), live end-to-end tests against real existing state (not clean repros), and a full checklist of completed verifications before any push/publish decision. They will not accept 'I think it's done' without the complete check list, and judge results by concrete evidence (screenshots, raw console output) rather than the assistant's assertion.
