## 1-7 · Dispatched research on tool-call arc closure mechanisms for compression eligibility
A Paseo subagent ("MC historian bughunt", id 379f3cad-431f-4c3f-a264-0d7ba358b831) was dispatched with a detailed research brief asking what concrete mechanisms exist (papers, OSS tools, agent harnesses) for detecting that a tool-call arc (call + result + model reaction) is FINISHED/consumed, as the eligibility gate for compressing or evicting the result bytes. The brief enumerated five signal classes to investigate: (1) dependency/consumption tracking (use-def provenance graphs), (2) step/turn boundary closure (ReFold "subtask finished" trigger, OpenHands condenser closure rule), (3) verify-cycle protection, (4) reference counting / tombstone patterns (MemGPT/Letta, Zep/Graphiti invalid_at, A-MEM), (5) OSS harness practice (Claude Code microcompact, Cursor/Windsurf/Cline compaction, aider checkpoint semantics).

I began systematic research: fetched the three named arXiv papers (StateComp 2609.27298, ReFold 2610.07863, Hidden Costs of Forgetting 2610.35496) — all three exist. Captured StateComp's READY predicate and ReFold's full text — ReFold's folding trigger is the agent's own `stale` self-report. OpenHands paths 404'd (org likely renamed). Claude Code's clear_tool_uses prompt and context-editing docs were being retried. The research was still in progress at the end of this chunk — locating OpenHands' actual repo and retrying Claude docs.

